    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Regarding the volume manipulation part though—how specifically do you recommend adjusting the sets and reps per week to avoid the plateau without just cutting everything out? Like, should I be looking at a drop in total tonnage or just changing the intensity of the working sets?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To avoid plateauing without cutting volume entirely, consider altering the rep scheme or weight distribution. For example, your client can decrease the number of sets or reps while maintaining or increasing the heavier weight, or shift to higher-volume, lower-weight sets to target non-major muscle groups. Additionally, you can try tiered stimuli with lighter warm-up sets and heavier working sets, reducing overall volume while still challenging the working muscles. Alternatively, cycling complete exercises or modes of training (e.g., swapping back to deadlifts after focusing on squats) can also serve as a deload without entirely losing training volume.",
      "source": "target",
